  "result": "I found the root cause, fixed it in `src/money.js`, and added regression tests. `npm test` passes with 7 of 7.\n\n**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` only accepted plain digits, like `12.50`. A price with a thousands separator, such as `\"2,450.50\"`, didn't match its regex. The code then read `m[1]` on a `null` match, which threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. That produced the 500s.\n\nEvery failing `POST /checkout` in `logs/app.log` has one of three comma-formatted prices:\n- `1,299.00` (15 times)\n- `2,450.50` (12 times)\n- `1,005.99` (10 times)\n\nOnly orders containing one of those prices failed, which is why the errors looked intermittent. No other price format in the log fails.\n\n**Fix:**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts properly grouped thousands, such as `1,299.00`, and strips the commas before parsing.\n- Malformed input like `abc`, `1,2` or `-5` now throws `Error('Invalid amount: …')`, as the function's own comment already promised. It no longer throws a null-dereference `TypeError`.\n\n**Tests:** I added three tests to `test/money.test.js`. They cover comma-formatted amounts, clear errors for bad input, and a `checkout` call with a comma price. The comma tests would have failed before the fix.\n\n**Not fixed:** At `08:38:44` the log shows a `99.99` × 1 order (`ord_11161`) returning `total=22160`, which looks wrong. I didn't investigate it, and it may be a separate bug.",
